  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe deadline for filing an estate tax return is within one year from the decedent's death. An extension of up to 30 days may be granted by the Commissioner in meritorious cases.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Section 89**: \"The estate tax return required... shall be filed within one (1) year from the decedent's death.\"\n- **NIRC Section 89**: \"The Commissioner shall have authority to grant, in meritorious cases, a reasonable extension not exceeding thirty (30) days for filing the return.\"\n\n### Interpretation\nThe law mandates that the estate tax return must be filed within one year of the death of the decedent. This timeframe is critical for compliance with tax obligations. The provision for a 30-day extension allows for some flexibility in cases where circumstances warrant additional time, but this extension is not automatic and must be justified.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, executors or administrators of an estate must be diligent in preparing and filing the estate tax return within the stipulated one-year period to avoid penalties. If they anticipate challenges in meeting this deadline, they should promptly seek an extension from the Commissioner, ensuring they provide valid reasons for the request. This proactive approach can help mitigate potential issues with tax compliance.",
